  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nOur Thanksgiving storm system has just about cleared the area \nwith the last of the rain scooting east of the CWA early this \nafternoon. As expected, the gusty NW winds had died down through\nthe morning as well and will continue to ease into the evening.\nThe back edge of the stratus is found over north-central IL and\nsome sunshine is breaking through over our western CWA. Most of\nus should see a fair amount of blue sky prior to dusk, maybe \nnot so much over in northwest Indiana. \n\nAdditional stratus will fill in from the west overnight as a \nsubtle upper wave scoots across the area. This wave is evident \non water vapor imagery pushing across the northern Plains with \nthe leading stratus extending into central IA. Both moisture and\nforcing will be too meager for appreciable precip development. \nA dry layer just off the deck will further inhibit any true \nshowers, and this layer gets drier with southward extent through\nthe CWA. However, still seeing a play at some sprinkles \novernight into very early Saturday. Accordingly, maintained a \nchance for sprinkles north of I-80 in the forecast. Surface \ntemperatures will be getting down to around or just below \nfreezing, but the sprinkle potential is generally not sparking \nany icing concerns. \n\nSome subtle low level warm advection in the wake of this system\nwill allow temperatures to get up to around 50 on Saturday. \nExpect mostly sunny skies after the early morning and a \nsouthwesterly breeze.\n\nAlso evident on water vapor imagery this afternoon is a \npositively tilted pacific trough pushing onshore the PNW coast. \nThis feature will dig sharply into the Plains on Saturday and \nacross the lower Midwest on Sunday. A stacked low will quickly \nspin up over the south central Plains and push across the OH \nvalley through the end of the weekend. As it does, a widespread \nprecip shield looks to lift into northern IL Saturday evening \nand night and rain is expected at the start of Sunday before \nretreating to the east during the latter part of the day. A \nprior signal for perhaps a brief change over to a rain/snow mix \non the backside of the precip Sunday evening has faded and the \nforecast calls for all liquid. This system should also bring \ngusty northwest winds to Sunday.\n\nIn the wake of this system, Monday morning temperatures should \nbe down well into the 20s followed by highs topping out on \neither side of 40. Between a surface high pushing to the east \nand a developing high-amplitude trough over the Plains to the \nwest, temperatures will be quick to recover with 40s forecast on\nTuesday and 50s back by Wednesday. Some spots may even make a \nrun at 60 Thursday and Friday. The first half of next week looks\nquiet while medium-range guidance favors another synoptic \nsystem cutting across the region in the Thursday to Friday \nwindow.\n\nDoom",
